
- vulkan shared memory (Trials fast path, G1): uploads of CPU-written pages
  are moved to the start of the submission (one batched copy and two
  barriers) instead of being recorded between draws, where each upload ended
  the render pass and added two barriers; falls back to the in-order upload
  when the GPU wrote the page earlier in the submission or bytes a draw
  already read changed (counted in the log_guest_fps line)
- vulkan resolves (Trials fast path, G3): 32bpp resolves are written straight
  into the image of the texture that samples the destination (new
  resolve_full_32bpp_image* shaders, an R32_UINT storage view of the texture)
  instead of the tiled guest layout that the texture cache untiled and copied
  into that image again; the texture stays up to date. Falls back to memory for
  depth, read-back, 64bpp and other resolves, and per destination if a texture
  ever loads memory such a resolve didn't write (counted in the log_guest_fps
  line)
- vulkan command processor (Trials fast path, C1): closed submissions are
  replayed into their command buffers and submitted on a "GPU Submit" thread
  (vulkan_async_submit), with frames split every vulkan_async_submit_chunk_draws
  draws so the replay overlaps recording; fences are assigned on close, and the
  swap submission and sparse binds wait for the thread to keep the queue order
  the presenter relies on
- graphics (Trials fast path, C18): with vsync off, a WAIT_REG_MEM on memory
  that isn't satisfied immediately asks the vblank thread for a guest vblank
  right away (GraphicsSystem::RequestVblank) instead of at the next 1 ms timer
  tick - Trials HD waits every frame for its vblank interrupt handler to
  release a semaphore (~0.5 ms of the command processor's frame)
- vulkan command processor: float constants are copied into the uniform
  buffers in runs of consecutive used constants instead of one by one (C2);
  texture cache: textures_generation(), and the G3 resolve target lookup is
  cached per destination until a texture is created or destroyed (C16)
- core (Linux): guest memory is backed by an anonymous memfd instead of a
  /dev/shm object (shm_open stays the fallback) - Docker's 64 MB /dev/shm made
  the guest arena fault with SIGBUS on first use
- vulkan_gpu_profile: "upload" category for the shared memory uploads hoisted
  to the start of the submission (they were counted as "other") and "transfer"
  for render target ownership transfer passes
- vulkan deferred command buffer (Trials fast path, G2): an EndRendering
  followed, with only state commands in between, by a BeginRendering of the
  same attachments is dropped together with it at replay (~31 per frame:
  mostly an ownership transfer into the target being drawn), counted as
  render_passes_merged (vulkan_merge_render_passes)
- vulkan resolves (Trials fast path, G12): a resolve into a texture image whose
  source region is owned by one render target with the resolve's own format,
  pitch and MSAA (1x / 4x 8_8_8_8, 2_10_10_10, k_16_16 through its uint
  transfer view) reads that render target directly
  (resolve_full_32bpp_image_rt*: the dump's addressing and packing per EDRAM
  word) instead of dumping it to the EDRAM buffer first
  (vulkan_resolve_from_render_target, resolves_from_rt in the log_guest_fps
  line); vulkan_gpu_profile lists pass time per render target configuration
- vulkan command processor (Trials fast path, C2): the guest draw constant
  buffers are dynamic uniform buffers with fixed ranges (float constants are
  given room for all 256), so their descriptor set only depends on which
  uniform pool pages hold them - cached per combination until the pools are
  cleared - and a draw only rebinds it with new offsets instead of allocating
  and writing a descriptor set (vulkan_dynamic_constant_buffers, init only)
- vulkan swap (Trials fast path, vulkan_async_swap): the command processor no
  longer waits at the swap for the submission thread to put the frame on the
  queue; the presenter's refresh fence is signaled through that thread
  (VulkanPresenter::AsyncQueueSubmitter) and the consumers of the guest output
  image (painting, capture) wait for it instead; the swap waits only until the
  presenter has taken the previous frame (Presenter::AwaitGuestOutputConsumed),
  so every frame is still presented
diff --git a/include/rex/audio/web/web_audio_driver.h b/include/rex/audio/web/web_audio_driver.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ca49f59
--- /dev/null
+++ b/include/rex/audio/web/web_audio_driver.h
@@ -0,0 +1,83 @@
+/**
+ * @file        audio/web/web_audio_driver.h
+ * @brief       Audio output of the web (Emscripten) build: a shared-memory ring
+ *              read by the page's AudioWorklet
+ *
+ * The drivers' frames (6-channel big-endian) are mixed to interleaved stereo
+ * and written into WebAudioRing, which lives in the wasm memory (a
+ * SharedArrayBuffer); the page's AudioWorklet (web/audio_worklet.js in Trials
+ * HD Recompiled) plays from it. The ring's fill paces the guest like the SDL
+ * device callback does natively: one client-semaphore release per frame
+ * mixed. While nothing plays the ring (no user gesture yet, a suspended
+ * AudioContext), frames are dropped at the playback rate instead so the
+ * game's audio threads keep their normal speed.
+ */
+
+#pragma once
+
+#include <rex/platform.h>
+
+#if !REX_PLATFORM_EMSCRIPTEN
+#error "rex/audio/web/web_audio_driver.h is for the web (Emscripten) build only"
+#endif
+
+#include <atomic>
+#include <cstddef>
+#include <cstdint>
+#include <deque>
+#include <mutex>
+#include <vector>
+
+#include <rex/audio/audio_driver.h>
+#include <rex/thread.h>
+
+namespace rex::audio::web {
+
+// Shared with web/audio_worklet.js; little-endian, offsets fixed.
+struct WebAudioRing {
+  static constexpr uint32_t kCapacityFrames = 8192;
+  static constexpr uint32_t kChannels = 2;
+
+  std::atomic<uint32_t> write_frame;  // 0: frames written in total (producer)
+  std::atomic<uint32_t> read_frame;   // 4: frames read in total (worklet)
+  uint32_t capacity_frames;           // 8
+  uint32_t sample_rate;               // 12
+  uint32_t channels;                  // 16
+  std::atomic<uint32_t> underruns;    // 20: worklet: quanta short of data while being fed
+  std::atomic<uint32_t> heartbeat;   // 24: worklet: +1 per render quantum while running
+  std::atomic<uint32_t> peak_milli;   // 28: worklet: max |sample| * 1000 played
+  float samples[kCapacityFrames * kChannels];  // 32: interleaved L R
+};
+static_assert(offsetof(WebAudioRing, samples) == 32);
+
+// The one ring (static storage, so inside the initial wasm memory).
+WebAudioRing& GetWebAudioRing();
+
+// One guest audio client. The game opens several (music, effects, ...);
+// natively the OS mixes their streams, here one mixer thread does: each
+// playback period it takes one queued frame from every client, sums them into
+// the ring and releases each of those clients' semaphores.
+class WebAudioDriver : public AudioDriver {
+ public:
+  WebAudioDriver(memory::Memory* memory, rex::thread::Semaphore* semaphore);
+  ~WebAudioDriver() override;
+
+  void SubmitFrame(uint32_t frame_ptr) override;
+
+  static constexpr uint32_t kFrameRate = 48000;
+  static constexpr uint32_t kGuestChannels = 6;
+  static constexpr uint32_t kChannelSamples = 256;
+  static constexpr uint32_t kFrameSamples = kGuestChannels * kChannelSamples;
+
+  // Mixer thread: adds this client's oldest queued frame (as stereo) to
+  // `mix` and releases its semaphore; false when nothing is queued.
+  bool MixFrame(float* mix, bool to_output);
+
+ private:
+  rex::thread::Semaphore* semaphore_;
+  std::mutex frames_mutex_;
+  std::deque<std::vector<float>> frames_queued_;
+  std::vector<std::vector<float>> frames_unused_;
+};
+
+}  // namespace rex::audio::web
